    689	### B4 — Voronin universality with effective shifts as a polylog approximator
    690	
    691	**Question:** Voronin (1975) proved that every non-vanishing analytic
    692	function `f` on a compact set `K ⊂ {1/2 < Re(s) < 1}` is uniformly
    693	approximable by `ζ(s + it)` for a positive-density set of vertical
    694	shifts `t`. Garunkstis 2003 gave the only known effective bound on
    695	the smallest valid `t`: `t ≤ exp(exp(10/ε^{13}))` — astronomical in
    696	the worst case. Question: are there *natural* target functions `f`
    697	(specifically, smooth approximants to `R^{-1}(n)` or `li(s)^{-1}`) for
    698	which the effective shift `t(f, ε)` scales POLYNOMIALLY in `1/ε`?
    699	If yes, evaluating `ζ(s + it)` at a single computable shift gives a
    700	polylog reconstruction of `f`, hence a polylog approximation to π(x).
    701	
    702	**Why frontier:** Voronin universality has NEVER been used
    703	algorithmically in published work — every existing application is
    704	existential. The empirical question "is the worst-case Garunkstis
    705	bound tight, or only an artefact of the proof?" is open. The
    706	project has done extensive effective-zero-truncation work (E3.2,
    707	E3.4, E3.5) but has never tried the universality angle, in which
    708	the goal is to *construct* a single ζ-value that evaluates the
    709	target rather than to *sum* contributions of many zeros.
    710	
    711	**Cross-domain ingredient:** Voronin universality theorem, Bohr
    712	almost-periodicity, joint universality of L-functions (Steuding
    713	2007), effective bounds (Garunkstis 2003).
