    868:| §P.P1 batched-on-modulus q amortisation, slot 3 — end-to-end Dirichlet-L zero finder using FFT-shared full AFE (main + reflected via conjugate-of-main identity) + Hardy Z function + sign-change bracketing + linear-interp refinement: does slot-2's L-eval primitive lift to a ≥3× single-query speedup on end-to-end π(x; q, a) computation? | REFINEMENT (architecture + bounded partial-positive at the FFT-vs-DIRECT axis) | I (slot-2's 5× projection FALSIFIED at 1.01×; FFT primitive structurally load-bearing for parity, slot 4-5 attack composite-q + Riemann-Siegel) | Thread 6 commit step 3/5 (S229). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/slot3_zero_finder_dirichlet.py` (~470 lines): full AFE main term M(t, χ) via slot-2 FFT primitive `phi · ifft(W_aggregate, axis=0)`; reflected term `W_χ · (q/π)^{-it} · ratio_gamma(t, a) · conj(M)` exploiting `Σ χ̄(n)/n^{1/2-it} = conj(Σ χ(n)/n^{1/2+it})` simplification for prime q (NO additional FFT — pointwise multiply); Gauss sums τ(χ_j) via FFT identity in single length-φ FFT; Hardy Z `Z_χ(t) = e^{i·θ(t)} · L(1/2+it, χ)` with `θ_χ(t) = (t/2)·log(q/π) + arg Γ((1/2+a+it)/2) - arg(W_χ)/2` via `np.imag(scipy.special.loggamma)`; vectorised sign-change bracketing across all (φ, n_t) Z values via `np.where(sign(Z[:,:-1]) * sign(Z[:,1:]) < 0)` + linear-interp refinement (9× speedup over per-character Python loop, project tooling fix). N_AFE oversized 12× (without Riemann-Siegel correction terms, balanced N gives error ~1 absolute breaking sign-change detection). Principal character (j=0) uses cached zeta-zeros directly. **Cross-validation against mpmath ground truth**: q=11, χ_1 first 5 zeros: slot-3 (3.55, 6.61, 7.88, 11.60, 13.31) vs mpmath (3.52, 6.64, 7.86, 11.60, 13.35), max diff 0.033; q=11, χ_5 (Legendre) first 4 zeros agree to 0.06 abs. **End-to-end timing q=1009, K=200, x=10⁶, 3-run median**: t_zerofind 151.5ms, t_partialsum 34.8ms, t_single 186.3ms; **1.01× parity vs slot-1 synthetic baseline (187.7ms)**. zeros[median per char] = 135 (out of K_target=200 — n_t=823 grid limited; not all close-spaced zeros captured). **HEADLINE — FFT vs DIRECT no-FFT-sharing**: q=101 K=200 t_max=198 n_t=849 N_afe=678: FFT 43.4ms vs DIRECT 41.1ms = 0.95× (FFT loses at small q); q=1009 K=200 t_max=153 n_t=823 N_afe=1883: **FFT 173.2ms vs DIRECT 352.9ms = 2.04× FFT speedup** (larger than slot-2's L-eval-only 1.13-1.79× because oversized N_afe=1883 widens FFT advantage at large N where BLAS saturates earlier than FFT). Crossover q ∈ [200, 500]. **Internal cost decomposition q=1009 K=200**: setup 0.3ms, AFE 139.3ms (73%), Hardy Z theta 28.2ms (15%), bracket 22.2ms (12%); AFE step dominates. **Amortised at M=256**: slot-3 FFT 35.4ms vs slot-1 23.4ms (slot-3 0.66× slot-1) — T_full_per_x dominated by partial-sum eval (135 real zeros 34.8ms vs slot-1 200 synthetic zeros 22.7ms), FFT primitive does NOT affect partial-sum eval, **exactly per E1.5 prediction**. **WHY slot-2's "5× single-query speedup" projection was wrong**: slot-1's synthetic generator is already cheap (Newton-on-density inversion vectorised numpy: 0.82 µs/gamma); real Dirichlet-L zeros via FFT-Hardy-Z cost 1.1 µs/gamma — only 1.34× more, NOT slower. Plus Hardy theta + bracket costs ~50ms unaccounted-for. **Structural finding (B-grade): real Dirichlet-L zeros for the price of synthetic density-inversion zeros, via cyclic DFT identity + truncated AFE main+reflected**. Not Correlation-Dichotomy-shaped (33× at M=64) but a non-trivial calibration of slot-2's primitive. **Falsifier list F3.1-F3.4 framing slots 4-5**: F3.1 Riemann-Siegel correction terms drop N_afe by 5-10× (Berry 1995 / Coffey 2003) → may push slot-3 from parity to 3-5× partial-positive; F3.2 composite-q multi-axis FFT for q = p₁p₂ (slot 4 PRIORITY-b); F3.3 cross-conductor batches (slot 5); F3.4 better FFT engines (FFTW3/cuFFT) close BLAS-vs-FFT gap. **Cross-domain ingredient**: Hardy Z function for Dirichlet L (Davenport "Multiplicative NT" §15-19 / Booker "Numerical computation of Riemann zeta function") USED I — first end-to-end Dirichlet-L zero finder via Hardy Z within the project; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §8 row added. Approximate Functional Equation full+reflected for primitive χ with the conjugate-identity simplification for prime q USED I. scipy.special.loggamma for complex argument (engineering, not new mathematics). Continuation of amortised algorithmics (Tarjan 1985 / Demaine-Patrascu 2008) USED I from Threads 5-6 slot 1-2. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content; amortised regime unchanged exactly per E1.5), E2.1 (spectral / sieve interface; cyclic DFT is dual-group spectral primitive), E3.1 (CCM setup-vs-eval decoupling; slot-3 confirms FFT primitive lifts setup but not eval), E6.6 (Aggarwal — orthogonal to slot 3; cross-call vs within-call amortisation), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template; slot 3 produces real-zeros-for-synthetic-cost shape, smaller than 33× pattern), S227 (slot 1 baseline matched at 1.01×), S228 (slot 2 primitive propagated end-to-end with quantitative falsification of 5× projection). See `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/{slot3_zero_finder_dirichlet.py, slot3_zero_finder_dirichlet_results.md, slot3_end_to_end.csv, slot3_fft_vs_direct.csv}`, `archive/sessions/session229_commit_p1_dirichlet_zero_finder.md`. | 229 |
    869:| §P.P1 batched-on-modulus q amortisation, slot 2 — AFE-shared L-value evaluation across characters mod q via cyclic DFT identity over (Z/qZ)*: does sharing the `1/n^{1/2+it}` table across all chi mod q give meaningful per-character cost reduction over optimised direct BLAS matmul? | REFINEMENT (operational confirmation + bounded partial-positive) | I (slot-2 falsifier α confirmed; wall-clock benefit bounded constant; slot 3-5 attack adjacent angles) | Thread 6 commit step 2/5 (S228). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/slot2_afe_shared_l_eval.py` — three implementations of truncated AFE main term `L(1/2+it, chi) ≈ Σ_{n ≤ N, gcd(n,q)=1} chi(n) / n^{1/2+it}`: DIRECT (`chi_at_n @ cp` via vectorised `char_table_at_residues` building only φ × N_co columns); AGGREGATE_MATMUL (build W[r, j] aggregate then `char_table @ W` of shape φ × φ — strictly worse than DIRECT at N_co < φ, skipped at q ≥ 5000 due to φ² memory); FFT (aggregate W in log-g order, then `phi · ifft(W, axis=0)` exploits cyclic DFT identity `χ_j(g^k) = ω^{jk}` to compute L for ALL φ chars at once per t). **Cross-method correctness**: max relative error vs DIRECT is ~1e-15 across q ∈ {11, 31, 101} (numerical floating-point limit). **HONEST cost profile** (12 rows q ∈ {101, 1009, 10007} × t_max ∈ {50, 200} × n_t ∈ {400, 800}): FFT wins in 11/12 configurations with median speedup 1.7× over OPTIMISED DIRECT, range 0.74×-6.35×. **Scaling profile** (q ∈ {101, 251, 503, 1009, 2003, 5003}, t_max=100, n_t=400): empirical FFT speedup is ESSENTIALLY FLAT in q at ~1.7×, while theoretical FLOP-ratio `N_co/log φ` grows from 5.8× (q=101) to 22.9× (q=5003). **Gap = BLAS hardware FLOP rate (~30 GFLOP/s, well-optimised) vs np.fft Bluestein (~3-5 GFLOP/s) — ratio ~8-12×**. End-to-end pi(x; q, a) implication: single-query speedup ~5× vs slot-1 synthetic baseline (188ms → ~33ms at q=1009, K=200, x=10⁶), but amortised regime UNCHANGED at slot-1 T_full_per_x = 22.7ms (set by phi(q) · K · 50ns partial-sum eval, which slot-2 primitive does NOT affect). **NOT a Correlation-Dichotomy-shaped partial-positive** (not the 33×-shaped result Thread 5 produced). Slot-1 falsifier (α) is structurally CORRECT but practically WEAK: cyclic DFT identity gives the right algebraic primitive, but BLAS hardware efficiency closes the wall-clock gap. **Methodological correction**: replaced slot-1-inherited `all_characters_table()` (φ² Python double loop, O(100s) overhead at q=10007) with vectorised `char_table_at_residues(q, residues)` (np.exp over outer-product (φ, len(residues))). Affects all future χ-side experiments. **Falsifier list F2.1-F2.4 framing slot 3+**: F2.1 alternate FFT impls (FFTW3 / cuFFT) may close BLAS-vs-FFT gap; F2.2 propagation to end-to-end zero-finding (slot 3 PRIORITY a); F2.3 composite-q via multi-axis FFT (slot 3 PRIORITY b); F2.4 better-tuned BLAS may widen the gap further. **Cross-domain ingredient**: FFT over the dual group of (Z/qZ)* (Bober-Hiary 2017 / Booker-Lobb 2009) USED I — first time in the project; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §10 row "FFT over Dirichlet character group" PROPOSED → USED I (constant-factor speedup confirmed but not sub-linear amortisation). Continuation of amortised algorithmics (Tarjan 1985 / Demaine-Patrascu 2008) USED I from Thread 5, now applied at the χ-axis with the cyclic DFT primitive. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content; amortised regime unchanged exactly per E1.5), E3.1 (Connes setup-vs-eval decoupling; slot-2 attacks setup), E2.1 (spectral / dual-group structure of (Z/qZ)*), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template — slot-2 implements the χ-axis analogue with bounded wall-clock benefit), S227 (slot-1 baseline directly extended). See `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/{slot2_afe_shared_l_eval.py, slot2_afe_shared_l_eval_results.md, slot2_l_eval_profile.csv, slot2_scaling.csv, slot2_scaling_quick.csv}`, `archive/sessions/session228_commit_p1_afe_shared_l_eval.md`. | 228 |
    870:| §P.P1 batched-on-modulus q amortisation, slot 5 (FINAL WRAP) — cross-conductor (Q-batched) amortisation: given Q = {q_1, ..., q_M} of distinct prime conductors, can the slot-3/4 pipeline share zero-finding work across distinct conductors to give per-(q, a) amortised polylog cost? | THEORY+EXPERIMENT (negative-shape closure of the fourth and final amortisation axis of P1) | E+I | Thread 6 commit step 5/5 FINAL (S231). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/slot5_cross_conductor_amortisation.py` (~400 lines): seven-stage decomposition of slot-3/4 pipeline (Stage A: cp_all = 1/n^{1/2+it} CONDUCTOR-INDEPENDENT; Stage B: aggregate cp_all by residue mod q PER-CONDUCTOR; Stage C: length-φ(q) FFT PER-CONDUCTOR; Stage D: reflected term + Gauss sums + W_χ PER-CONDUCTOR; Stage E: loggamma at t-grid CONDUCTOR-INDEPENDENT; Stage F: Hardy θ + Z PER-CONDUCTOR; Stage G: sign-change bracket PER-CONDUCTOR). Two pipelines: INDEPENDENT (rebuild Stage A+E inside q-loop) vs Q-BATCHED-SHARED (build Stage A sized to N_max + Stage E once before q-loop). **Numerical equivalence verified**: max abs diff between zero databases = 0.000e+00 at Q={101, 251, 503, 1009} K=5; Stage-A sharing introduces NO accuracy loss. **M-sweep K=200 n_t=823 t_max=200 family Q⊆{1009, 2003, 5003, 10007}**: M=1 (1009): T_indep 264.3ms vs T_batch 134.5ms, speedup 1.97× [ARTEFACT — pipelines functionally identical at M=1, ratio is cold-start FFT-planner / numpy allocation effect with indep called first per run-trio]; M=2: 485.5/389.7ms = 1.25×; M=3: 1267.4/1133.3ms = 1.12×; M=4: 3493.2/3341.4ms = 1.05×. **Speedup decreases monotonically toward 1× as M grows** — OPPOSITE shape from S224 Correlation Dichotomy (33× at M=64, INCREASING in M). Stage A breakdown M=4: indep_stageA_total=572ms, batch_stageA_once=343ms, saving 229ms (4.4% of total); Stage E saving 1ms (sub-dominant by 150×). **Theoretical Lemma (cross-conductor amortisation bounded constant)**: for Q = {q_1, ..., q_M} of distinct prime conductors, the slot-3-style pipeline admits T_batch/T_indep ≥ 1 - c·(T_A(N_max) + T_E)/T_indep(Q). Stages B, C, D, F, G all per-conductor by structural argument: (i) Stage B residue map n → n mod q is q-dependent; no primitive aggregates 1/n^{1/2+it} into ALL φ(q_i) characters of all q_i in Q simultaneously (distinct primes have COPRIME multiplicative groups (Z/qZ)* of order q-1 with no nontrivial common refinement; CRT (Z/q_1q_2)* ≅ (Z/q_1)* × (Z/q_2)* requires SINGLE composite modulus, not a *family*); (ii) Stages C, F, G have FFT length φ(q_i), q-dependent; (iii) Stage D has Gauss sums and root numbers depending on q. Saving is bounded by T_A(N_max) + T_E = O(N_max·n_t + n_t), one constant. Total per-conductor work grows as M·√q_avg·log q_avg·n_t. saving/total → 0 as M·√q_avg grows. ∎. **Corollary**: Q-axis admits NO Correlation-Dichotomy-shaped partial-positive on slot-3/4 architecture. **Thread 6 final verdict — P1 closes negatively across all four amortisation axes**: AXIS 1 (a-direction, slot 1, S227): trivial 8× at M=256 (zeros independent of a, exactly per E1.5); AXIS 2 (χ-direction fixed q, slots 2-3, S228+S229): bounded constant 1.79-2.04× via cyclic DFT primitive (BLAS-vs-Bluestein FFT FLOP rate); AXIS 3 (composite q, slot 4, S230): bounded constant 1.75× via multi-axis FFT (same crossover); AXIS 4 (Q-batched cross-conductor, slot 5, S231): bounded constant decreasing toward 1× as M grows. **None structural in algorithmic-complexity sense; all hardware-level FFT/BLAS arithmetic.** Thread 6 produces no batch-polylog primes-in-AP algorithm via explicit-formula route. **Thread-5-vs-Thread-6 distinction (NEW project artefact)**: Thread 5 partial-positive (S224) operated on SHARED-L geometry — same ζ-zero database across queries. Thread 6 fails on DISTINCT-L geometry — distinct L(s, χ_q) per query, no shared zero database across distinct conductors. Future P_x targets should prioritise shared-L shapes over distinct-L shapes when seeking partial-positives. **Successor thread recommendations (slot-5 self-extension)**: Thread 7 = P3 (approximate π(x) ± ε in polylog with named ε; smallest cross-domain barrier, strongest a-priori chance of partial-positive based on R(x) error analysis with K=log²x zeros heuristically yielding ε ≈ √x · log log x / log⁴ x); Thread 8 = P2 (π_h(x) batched on h; shared-h-singular-series structure closer to Thread 5 shape); Thread 9 = P9 (quantum batched primes-in-AP; possibly different scaling). **Falsifiers F5.1-F5.5**: F5.1 pipeline equivalence checked (max abs diff = 0e0); F5.2 speedup decreases monotonically (1.25 → 1.12 → 1.05) NOT sub-linear in M; F5.3 M=1 row 1.97× is timing artefact (cold-start FFT-planner with indep first per run); F5.4 ALTERNATIVE attack via Selberg trace formula or Iwaniec-Kowalski large-sieve NOT investigated (different cross-domain imports could change the lemma); F5.5 quantum batched amortisation NOT investigated (potential Thread 9). **Cross-domain ingredient**: NONE NEW. Slot 5 is rigorous-experimentation + algebraic-structure argument applied to existing primitives (slot-3/4 architecture). The slot-5 result is theoretical lemma + empirical confirmation, NOT new technique import — appropriate for wrap session. CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §10 cyclic/multi-axis DFT family entries are now complete with the slot-5 negative-shape: family does NOT extend to a cross-conductor primitive. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content; each axis confirms E1.5), E2.1 (spectral/sieve; cyclic and multi-axis DFT primitives), E3.1 (CCM setup-vs-eval decoupling applied to (A,E) vs (B-G)), E6.6 (Aggarwal binary search analogue), E6.7 (Deléglise-Rivat orthogonal pillar), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template; slot-5 produces Q-axis analogue with OPPOSITE shape), S190 (Thread 1 closed), S202 (Thread 3 closed), S215 (Thread 4 closed) — Thread 6 joins as fifth structural closure, S227-S230 (slots 1-4). See `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/{slot5_cross_conductor_amortisation.py, slot5_cross_conductor_amortisation_results.md, slot5_cross_conductor.csv}`, `archive/sessions/session231_commit_p1_cross_conductor_wrap.md`. | 231 |
    871:| §P.P1 batched-on-modulus q amortisation, slot 4 — composite-q multi-axis FFT primitive via CRT decomposition + end-to-end Dirichlet-L zero finder for squarefree odd composite q: does the multi-axis primitive (numpy.fft.ifftn over a CRT-tuple-indexed tensor) extend slot-3's structural lift to the practical AP regime, and does composite q introduce asymptotic improvement over prime q? | REFINEMENT (architecture extension + bounded constant lift preserved) | I (multi-axis FFT works; FFT vs DIRECT 1.75× at q=1001 vs slot-3's 2.04× at q=1009; no asymptotic gain at composite q) | Thread 6 commit step 4/5 (S230). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/slot4_composite_q_multi_axis_fft.py` (~600 lines): CRT decomposition `crt_decomp(q)` returns (primes, group_orders [p_i-1], primitive_roots g_i mod p_i) for squarefree odd q; `build_crt_log_table(q, ...)` precomputes residue → CRT-tuple (k_1, ..., k_K) where n ≡ g_i^{k_i} (mod p_i); multi-axis FFT primitive `l_eval_multiaxis_fft` aggregates W of shape (p_1-1, ..., p_K-1, n_t) and applies `phi · numpy.fft.ifftn(W, axes=(0,...,K-1))` to recover L-values for ALL φ(q) characters via dual-group identity `χ_{j_1,...,j_K}(n) = Π_i ω_i^{j_i k_i}`; full AFE main + reflected via same `Σ χ̄(n)/n^{½-it} = conj(Σ χ(n)/n^{½+it})` simplification (holds for ALL primitive characters of finite abelian groups, not just prime q); Gauss sums τ(χ) via multi-axis FFT identity over e_a; χ(-1) parity formula (Σ_i j_i) mod 2 [BUG-FIX: initial implementation used wrong (j_i · (p_i-1)/2) mod 2 which only matched prime q; caught via mpmath cross-check]; primitive character flag (j_i ≠ 0 ∀ i for squarefree q); Hardy Z + sign-change bracketing identical to slot 3. **Cross-method correctness verified to 1e-15 relative error** at q ∈ {15, 35, 105, 1001} via direct per-character matmul `chi_table @ cp` reference. **mpmath ground-truth zero finder cross-check** at q=15 χ_index=(1,1) t_max=40: slot-4 (2.697, 5.229, 8.383, 10.232, 11.896) vs mpmath Hardy-Z ground truth (2.746, 5.238, 8.418, 10.194, 11.920), max abs diff 0.049; q=35 χ_index=(1,1) t_max=30: max abs diff 0.062 — consistent with slot-3's 0.033 at q=11. **End-to-end π(x; q, a) timing 3-run median (q, factors, φ, K, t_single)**: (15, [3,5], 8, 50, 3.96ms), (35, [5,7], 24, 50, 5.25ms), (105, [3,5,7], 48, 50, 6.94ms), (105, [3,5,7], 48, 200, 38.4ms), (1001, [7,11,13], 720, 200, 166.6ms). **HEADLINE COMPARISON**: q=1009 (prime, φ=1008, slot-3, K=200): 186.3ms vs q=1001 (composite, φ=720, slot-4, K=200): 166.6ms — 11% wall-clock drop at q=1001 attributable mostly to smaller φ (29% reduction), NOT multi-axis-FFT-specific; per-character cost essentially unchanged because N_AFE depends on q (not φ) and is comparable between q=1001 and q=1009. **FFT vs DIRECT (no FFT sharing) within slot-4 pipeline**: q=105 K=50 N_AFE=283 n_t=218: FFT 5.09ms vs DIRECT 4.24ms = 0.83× (FFT loses, small composite q); q=1001 K=200 N_AFE=1872 n_t=823: FFT 145.24ms vs DIRECT 254.39ms = **1.75× FFT speedup** (vs slot-3's 2.04× at prime q=1009). **Multi-axis FFT preserves slot-3's structural lift in composite-q regime; does NOT introduce asymptotic gain.** Crossover q ∈ [200, 500] same as slot-2/slot-3 prime-q. Multi-axis FFT slightly underperforms single-axis at comparable q because numpy.fft.ifftn calls 1D FFT routines per axis; small per-axis transforms (e.g., shape (6, 10, 12) at q=1001) have proportionally higher constant overhead than one large 1D FFT (size 1008 at q=1009). **Falsifiers F4.1-F4.4 framing slot 5**: F4.1 multi-axis FFT does not give asymptotic improvement over slot-3 single-axis at composite q (constant-factor lift in same crossover regime); F4.2 end-to-end wall-clock dominated by AFE eval (N_AFE · n_t depends on q not φ, composite q inherits same AFE cost as nearby prime q); F4.3 multi-axis FFT does not shrink AFE truncation requirement (balanced N = √(qt/(2π)) is q-only); F4.4 cross-conductor amortisation across Q-family NOT attempted in slot 4 (slot-3 leftover priority-a). **Cross-domain ingredient**: CRT-based multi-axis Dirichlet character DFT — generalisation of slot-2's cyclic-group DFT primitive (Bober-Hiary 2017) to direct-product groups (Z/p_1Z)* ⊕ ... ⊕ (Z/p_KZ)* via Chinese Remainder Theorem (Davenport "Multiplicative NT" §1). Implemented as numpy.fft.ifftn on multi-dimensional tensor indexed by CRT log-tuples. **NEW USED I** in this project; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §10 extension "CRT multi-axis Dirichlet character DFT". Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content barrier; multi-axis primitive affects setup + AFE eval but not partial-sum eval, exactly per E1.5), E2.1 (spectral / sieve interface; multi-axis DFT generalises slot-2's cyclic DFT to direct-product groups), E3.1 (CCM setup-vs-eval decoupling; multi-axis FFT lifts setup but not eval), E6.6 (Aggarwal — orthogonal, within-q vs cross-call), E6.7 (DR per-query Θ(x^{2/3}/log²) — orthogonal pillar), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template; slot-4 wall-clock shape ~1.75× constant lift NOT Correlation-Dichotomy-shaped, same shape as slot-3 "real-zeros for synthetic-cost"), S227 (slot 1), S228 (slot 2 cyclic-DFT primitive that slot-4 generalises), S229 (slot 3 prime-q end-to-end pipeline that slot-4 extends). See `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/{slot4_composite_q_multi_axis_fft.py, slot4_composite_q_multi_axis_fft_results.md, slot4_composite_q_end_to_end.csv, slot4_composite_fft_vs_direct.csv}`, `archive/sessions/session230_commit_p1_composite_q_multi_axis_fft.md`. | 230 |
    873:| §1.C9.b.iv composition challenge — four-point identity for spike-pointwise pointwise approximator at primorial conductor: do the (S205, S208, S209) k=(2,1,3)-point HL singular series identities extend to k=4 with the same proof template? | REFINEMENT (closed-form 4-point identity proven analytically + verified empirically) | E | S234 construction-mode session (C9.b.iv). Built `experiments/constructions/spike_pointwise_HL_quad/tq_quad_correlation.py` (~370 lines) with seven pre-stated falsifiers (F1-F7). **Theorem (S234, proven analytically; verified to 0.06% across 5×11 admissible cells at d=20):** for every squarefree W ≥ 1 and integers (h_1, h_2, h_3), `<T_W^{div}(n) T_W^{div}(n+h_1) T_W^{div}(n+h_2) T_W^{div}(n+h_3)>_n = (π(N)/N)^4 · ∏_{p|W} (p − ν_p(0,h_1,h_2,h_3)) p^3/(p−1)^4 = (π(N)/N)^4 · S_HL^{(W)}(0,h_1,h_2,h_3)`. **Two independent proofs**: (a) direct from S208 pointwise collapse `T_W^{div} = (π/N)·(W/φ(W))·[gcd(n,W)=1]` + CRT density factorisation across primes p|W giving `<∏[gcd(n+h_i,W)=1]>_n = ∏_{p|W} (p−ν_p)/p`; (b) Ramanujan-Fourier 4-cumulant expansion `G_p^{(4)} = 1 + 1/(p-1)² Σ_{i<j} c_p(h_j-h_i) − 1/(p-1)³ Σ_{|S|=3} f_p^{(3)}(S) + 1/(p-1)^4 f_p^{(4)}(h_1,h_2,h_3)` algebraically equal to `(p−ν_p) p^3/(p-1)^4` across 78/78 cells (p ∈ {2,3,5,7,11,13} × 13 non-zero shift triples) at abs_err ≤ 4.44e-16. **Closed form by ν_p**: ν_p=1 → (p/(p-1))^3; ν_p=2 → (p-2)p^3/(p-1)^4; ν_p=3 → (p-3)p^3/(p-1)^4; ν_p=4 → (p-4)p^3/(p-1)^4; ν_p=p (inadmissible) → 0. **Reduction lemmas (F4/F5 algebraic)**: h_1=h_2=h_3=0 reduces to `<T_W^4>=(π/N)^4(W/φ(W))^3`; h_3=0 reduces to `(W/φ(W)) · S209-3pt`; h_2=h_3=0 reduces to `(W/φ(W))² · S205-2pt`. **Falsifier verdict**: F1 PASS dramatically (worst 0.060% at d=20 W=2310 cell (4,6,10), 100× tighter than pre-stated 0.5% band); F2 PARTIAL (4/6 admissible cells within pre-stated 2.5% at d=20 Q=2310, worst 5.7% at (6,10,12) — cross-conductor leakage at k=4 amplified by 4-6× vs S205 k=2 due to 3 disconnected pair contractions); F3 PARTIAL (4/6 within 3% at d=20, worst 3.79% at (2,8,14)); F4 PASS (1.00000); F5 PASS (1.00000 at h_3=0 reductions); F6 PASS (inadmissible (2,4,6)/(2,4,8) primorial-W ⊇ {3} → 0 exactly; (2,4,8) finite-N edge effect 1/N decay confirmed); F7 PASS (78/78 cells, machine precision). **Composes S205 (2-point) + S208 (1-point divisor collapse) + S209 (3-point) + E2.13 (Gowers U^2 cube — 4-tuple (0,h_1,h_2,h_1+h_2) at e.g. (2,6,8) gives U^2 norm closed form) + E2.16 (3-point HL prime factorisation extended to 4-tuples) + E1.6/E2.2 (parity bisection at q=2; admissibility forces all h_i even) + E2.1 (spike pointwise 4-point form). **Closes the (S205, S208, S209, S234) k = (1, 2, 3, 4) hierarchy.** General-k closed form `G_p^{(k)} = (p − ν_p) p^{k-1}/(p-1)^k` follows by induction (proposed C9.b.v). **NO polylog opening** (cost is `O(N · |H|^3)` for general k=4 sweep; closed-form prediction `O(ω(W))` per cell). **No new edge** — refines E2.1 inline; refinement of E2.13 (extends 2-point HL imprint to 4-point), E2.16 (positive identity from negative-shape DPP failure). **Cross-domain ingredient**: NONE NEW (project-internal: Ramanujan sums + Möbius inversion + HL singular series + CRT density factorisation). CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md unchanged. **Successor challenges (proposed in S234)**: C9.b.iv.α (cross-conductor leakage at k=4 closed-form bound — explains the 5.7% F2 deviation); C9.b.iv.β (Lean formalisation of the 4-point identity at primorial W — three-line proof tractable); C9.b.v (general-k inductive closed form proof); C9.b.vii (Hoffman / triple-MZV interpretation of f_p^{(k)} per multiplicity profile). Cites E2.1 (annotated S234 inline), E2.13 (HL imprint at U^2 cube), E2.16 (3-point HL prime factorisation), E1.6/E2.2 (parity), S191 (C9 spike pointwise), S205 (2-point), S208 (divisor collapse), S209 (3-point). See `experiments/constructions/spike_pointwise_HL_quad/{tq_quad_correlation.py, tq_quad_correlation_results.md, tq_quad_correlation_results.json, defini